  "query_preview": "..With deep moisture and modest instability in place\nacross northern and central Utah this afternoon, convection is \nexpected to redevelop through this evening. Across northern Utah, \ncoverage is expected to be more isolated given the convective\nactivity that already moved through this morning. However, recent\nmesoanalysis indicates that greatest instability and shear will \nbe focused across the northern Wasatch Front this afternoon and \nevening with 500-1000 J/kg of MLCAPE and about 30-40 kts of\neffective shear. As such, any storms that can develop through \nthis evening will be capable of sustaining updrafts that would be\ncapable of producing an isolated wind gust in excess of 58 mph and\nhail greater than 1 inch. Across east-central Utah, more modest\ninstability will exist with much deeper moisture (PWATs 1.0-1.2\")\nin place. As such, storms in this area will be capable of\nproducing heavy rainfall, with rainfall rates of 0.5-1+\" per hour\npossible. Given recent rainfall across portions of central Utah,\nespecially the San Rafael Swell and Wasatch Plateau, conditions\nare favorable for flash flooding across these areas. \n\nConvective activity will wane late this evening and overnight, \nbut lingering instability associated with a closed low pressure \nsystem moving eastward across the Great Basin may allow showers \nand thunderstorms reinvigorate across northern Utah early Friday\nmorning. A vorticity lobe ejects through the northern half of the\nstate late Friday morning, maximizing instability across the \nregion and resulting in increasing coverage of convection across\nnorthern and central Utah and southwest Wyoming through the \nafternoon. Conditions will once again be favorable for severe \nwind and hail across northern Utah. \n\nConvection clears out late Friday night, with drier conditions\nprevailing through Saturday behind the closed low. Much cooler\nconditions are expected to give us another taste of fall on Saturday,\nwith highs around 10 degrees below average. This cooler and drier\ntrend won't stick around long as another low pressure system digs\ndown the coast of southern California on Monday, yielding moist,\nsoutherly flow across the region which will bring increased\nchances for showers and thunderstorms mainly across southwest Utah\nearly next week.",
